    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="49" relname="span">I have something to add to the part of your discussion that says:</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="28" relname="span">The only way to ascertain user expectations is to do user testing.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="2" relname="elaboration">No amount of study and debate will substitute.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="48" relname="span">I was reading an article that Jackob Nielson wrote on heurisitics</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="4" relname="elaboration">and ran across an interesting statement.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="29" relname="span">Nielson (1992) said that minor usability problems could be real usabiltiy problems that could not be observed in a user test (p. 378).</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="30" relname="span">I could see how problems and expectations might cross into a grey area at times,</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="31" relname="span">and would say that both heuristics(or other type of expert inspection) and user testing would be needed to ascertain user expectations in the long view of a product's development.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="32" relname="span">An example might be where users are brought in to test a web interface.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="42" relname="span">Users are expecting a quick checkout to purchase something.</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="12" relname="concession">The first user really have to focus on entering in his credit card information correctly,</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="44" relname="span">but  he succeeds.</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="46" relname="concession">The next user has to focus just as hard</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="46" relname="span">but the transcation itself is perceived as quick by the user</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="14" relname="elaboration">and he only views the credit card entry as something you have to be careful in entering.</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="42" relname="elaboration">Both users had to enter the credit card number as one long number like xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx.</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="41" relname="elaboration">User expectations are met and the evaluators are none the wiser.</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="39" relname="span">Now I come in as an expert reviewer,</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="18" relname="elaboration">and I am evaluating the credit card entry process.</segment>
        <segment id="20" parent="36" relname="span">I might say that there should be dashes in between the blank area to enter my credit card</segment>
        <segment id="21" parent="38" relname="span">becuase this will visually match what a user sees on his credit card when typing it in</segment>
        <segment id="22" parent="21" relname="elaboration">(heuristic where thre is a match between the system and the real world).</segment>
        <segment id="23" parent="38" relname="elaboration">It would look like xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx instead of xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx for data entry.</segment>
        <segment id="24" parent="40" relname="span">I just went to a major department store website, nordstrom.com, to see if they put dashes for their customers' credit card entry area.</segment>
        <segment id="25" parent="24" relname="volitional-result">They don't.</segment>
        <group id="26" type="span"/>
